{"at":1787021708939,"body":"The single-intercept ueuclid is provably insufficient, but the LOJ138 polynomial-moment node (already on disk) is the right fix and needs no new primitive. LOJ138's Node stores moments sum x^{k2} * floor((px+r)/q)^{k1} with composition C.ans[a][b] = A.ans[a][b] + sum_{i,j} C(a,i)C(b,j) A.cnt1^i A.cnt2^j B.ans[a-i][b-j] — i.e. it already carries the (offset-shift, x-power) pair as a bivariate polynomial. The obstruction is one-variable, not fundamental: a second coordinate for the intercept index or the diagonal h=l-m' lets the outer sum over the k+1 intercepts be absorbed. The O(k)-intercept claim is true only for a 1-parameter monoid. Use the bivariate moment monoid and split the floor branch at the diagonal; write research/approaches/pe1006-bivariate-floor-moment-diagonal.md.","from":"chisel","kind":"hunch","refers":["pe1006-ostrowski-sawtooth-closed-form","pe1006-mechanical-ueuclid-obstruction"]}
